To further validate the proteomic analyses on fibrinogen and prothrombin levels, we measured fibrinogen, prothrombin, and D-dimer concentration from the vaccinated, non-vaccinated COVID, as well as healthy BALF samples by ELISA (
Fig. S4
). Unlike their plasma concentrations, which varied less than threefold among individuals, the BALF fibrinogen and prothrombin concentrations varied up to 500-fold (
Fig. 4A
;
Fig. S4
). Overall, most fibrinogen concentrations varied from less than 50 to 500 ng/mL in the vaccinated, but to greater than 50 µg/mL in non-vaccinated COVID BALF samples. Likewise, most prothrombin concentrations ranged from 10 to 200 ng/mL in vaccinated BALF but to ~3 µg/mL in non-vaccinated samples (
Fig. 4A
;
Fig. S4
). Consistent with the mass spectrometry data, concentrations from vaccinated individuals are similar to those from healthy donors but are significantly lower than those from non-vaccinated individuals (
Fig. 4A
). When the vaccinated and non-vaccinated samples are further separated into the acute and recovery phases, it shows that vaccination primarily reduced the acute phase coagulation factor concentrations (
Fig. 4B
). Despite their wide variation in concentrations, both prothrombin and D-dimer levels are correlated with those of fibrinogen (
Fig. 4C
).
